2.  THE SENSITIVITY: how much alpha does one part in a hundred of gamma_v buy?
----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
  primary /canonical : at alpha = 1,  d gamma_iso/d log alpha = -0.5374,  d gamma_perp/d log alpha = -0.5866
            -> the frozen sigma_tot = 0.028 on the aggregate (isotropic) statistic measures alpha to 0.052 dex = a factor 1.13
            -> the ORIENTATION-RESOLVED perpendicular bin, at the same per-bin sigma, measures alpha to 0.048 dex = a factor 1.12
  primary /alt       : at alpha = 1,  d gamma_iso/d log alpha = -0.5698,  d gamma_perp/d log alpha = -0.6136
            -> the frozen sigma_tot = 0.028 on the aggregate (isotropic) statistic measures alpha to 0.049 dex = a factor 1.12
            -> the ORIENTATION-RESOLVED perpendicular bin, at the same per-bin sigma, measures alpha to 0.046 dex = a factor 1.11
  alt     /canonical : at alpha = 1,  d gamma_iso/d log alpha = -0.5082,  d gamma_perp/d log alpha = -0.5619
            -> the frozen sigma_tot = 0.028 on the aggregate (isotropic) statistic measures alpha to 0.055 dex = a factor 1.14
            -> the ORIENTATION-RESOLVED perpendicular bin, at the same per-bin sigma, measures alpha to 0.050 dex = a factor 1.12
  alt     /alt       : at alpha = 1,  d gamma_iso/d log alpha = -0.5432,  d gamma_perp/d log alpha = -0.5915
            -> the frozen sigma_tot = 0.028 on the aggregate (isotropic) statistic measures alpha to 0.052 dex = a factor 1.13
            -> the ORIENTATION-RESOLVED perpendicular bin, at the same per-bin sigma, measures alpha to 0.047 dex = a factor 1.12
  [PASS] 2a CAN FAIL: does the frozen wide-binary statistic have enough sensitivity to MEASURE the kernel width -- i.e. to separate alpha = 1 from alpha = 0.5 and from alpha = 2, which is a factor 2 either way?   (sigma(log alpha) = 0.052 dex from sigma(gamma_v) = 0.028; a factor 2 is 0.301 dex)

