3.  THE UPSILON LEVER -- the point of the item
----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
    The eigenvalues (K3a, K3b) contain g_ext, a_0 and alpha.  They do not contain the binary's mass, its light,
    its distance or its inclination.  So d log alpha / d log Upsilon = 0 EXACTLY in the PREDICTION.
    Stellar mass re-enters only in the MEASUREMENT, which compares an observed relative velocity with the
    Newtonian one: gamma_v = v_obs/v_Newton and v_Newton ~ sqrt(M), so d log gamma_v/d log M = -1/2 exactly.
  d gamma_v/d log M = -0.5561;  d gamma_v/d log alpha = -0.5374
  => d log alpha / d log M_star = +1.035   -- against the galactic items' d log alpha/d log Upsilon = +0.74, this is WORSE per dex, but the calibrator is different in kind
      A galaxy's Upsilon_[3.6] is uncertain by 0.10-0.25 dex; a main-sequence photometric mass from Gaia colours is uncertain by about 0.02-0.04 dex, and it is calibrated on ECLIPSING BINARIES -- i.e. on masses measured by gravity itself, not by a stellar-population model.
      a 0.02 dex mass calibration gives sigma(log alpha) = 0.021 dex from the mass alone (a factor 1.05 in alpha)
      a 0.04 dex mass calibration gives sigma(log alpha) = 0.041 dex from the mass alone (a factor 1.10 in alpha)
      a 0.10 dex mass calibration gives sigma(log alpha) = 0.103 dex from the mass alone (a factor 1.27 in alpha)
  [PASS] 3a THE DECIDING COMPARISON: the wide-binary route's mass-calibration contribution to sigma(log alpha) must beat the galactic route's, where a 0.10-0.25 dex Upsilon uncertainty times a 0.74 lever gives 0.074-0.185 dex   (wide binaries at a 0.04 dex mass calibration: 0.041 dex; SPARC at a 0.10 dex Upsilon: 0.074 dex; SPARC at 0.25 dex: 0.185 dex)

4.  WHAT THE EXISTING (CONTESTED) DR3 NUMBERS WOULD IMPLY, stated as a range and not as a result
----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
    The DR3 literature does not agree: one analysis reports an aggregate boost near 1.16-1.23 at wide
    separations and another reports 1.00 (Newtonian).  Both are quoted here and NEITHER is adopted.
    gamma_v(aggregate) = 1.00  ->  alpha = 2.53, 2.90, 2.36, 2.61   (primary/alt g_ext x canonical/alt a_0)
    gamma_v(aggregate) = 1.05  ->  alpha = 1.42, 1.61, 1.29, 1.44   (primary/alt g_ext x canonical/alt a_0)
    gamma_v(aggregate) = 1.08  ->  alpha = 1.17, 1.31, 1.08, 1.19   (primary/alt g_ext x canonical/alt a_0)
    gamma_v(aggregate) = 1.12  ->  alpha = 0.97, 1.07, 0.90, 0.98   (primary/alt g_ext x canonical/alt a_0)
    gamma_v(aggregate) = 1.16  ->  alpha = 0.84, 0.91, 0.79, 0.85   (primary/alt g_ext x canonical/alt a_0)
    gamma_v(aggregate) = 1.20  ->  alpha = 0.74, 0.80, 0.71, 0.75   (primary/alt g_ext x canonical/alt a_0)
  [PASS] 4a CAN FAIL, AND IT IS THE HONEST STATE OF THE ARM: the two competing DR3 readings must give DIFFERENT alphas, or the statistic would not be measuring the width at all   (gamma_v = 1.00 is off the bottom of the family entirely (no alpha reproduces a Newtonian boost in an EFE-quenched MOND kernel); gamma_v = 1.20 needs alpha well below 1.  The arm is decisive and it is not yet decided)

