  Route A's own values, for the record: y* = 2.5398, max(g_obs-g_bar) = 0.64761 a_0 = 6.062e-11 / 7.318e-11 m/s^2, g_obs/g_bar = 1.2550 there
  AGAINST INTEREST: the landmark is only OBSERVATIONALLY reachable for alpha >~ 0.8, because below that y* runs out to y = 45 and beyond, where no rotation curve has the M/L control to see it.  So the landmark arm CONFIRMS a narrow kernel but cannot exclude a broad one; the wide-binary arm is the two-sided one.

    ONE FACT ABOUT THE FROZEN TARGET, RECORDED AND NOT ACTED ON.  The pre-registration's wide-binary target
    was computed with nu = sqrt(1 + 1/y), which is NOT the operative Route A kernel.  On the same frozen g_ext
    and the canonical footing, the sqrt kernel gives gamma_iso = 1.0799 (inside the frozen band 1.05-1.10)
    while Route A (alpha = 1) gives 1.1122, which is ABOVE it by 1.2 sigma_tot.  That is a kernel-choice difference of the same
    size as the measurement error, and it is recorded here for the programme to decide.  NOTHING FROZEN IS
    MODIFIED BY THIS SCRIPT: it reads the frozen numbers and writes none.

6.  MUTATION CONTROLS
----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
  [PASS] M1 a_0 x 4 must move gamma_v -- the statistic is not a_0-blind, which is also why it cannot measure alpha and a_0 at once (the a_0-degeneracy flag on the frozen pre-registration says exactly this)   (gamma_iso(4 a_0) = 1.3374 vs 1.1122)
  [PASS] M2 with nu = 1 (no kernel) both eigenvalues must be exactly 1 and gamma_v must be exactly Newtonian   (nu=1 gives par 1.000000, perp 1.000000)
  NEWTONIAN/LambdaCDM alternative, computed beside it: gamma_v = 1.0000 exactly at every separation and every
  orientation, with no free parameter -- there is no dark matter on 10 kAU scales in any halo model.

======================================================================================================================
VERDICT -- k03
======================================================================================================================
  gamma_v is a monotone, Upsilon-free function of the kernel width: at the frozen g_ext and the canonical
  footing the isotropic boost runs 1.5507 (alpha = 0.4) -> 1.1122 (alpha = 1, Route A) -> 0.9954 (alpha = 3).
  d gamma_v/d log alpha = -0.5374, so the frozen sigma_tot = 0.028 measures alpha to 0.052 dex.
  The mass-calibration lever is d log alpha/d log M = 1.03, which at a 0.04 dex photometric-mass
  calibration contributes 0.041 dex -- against 0.111 dex for the SPARC route at a
  0.15 dex Upsilon.  This is the Upsilon-free measurement of the kernel's second constant.

